// document-demo — a comprehensive tour of the `document` package.
//
// The `document` package layers a LaTeX-`article`-style format over the texish
// engine: a title block, an abstract, numbered sectioning, a table of contents,
// lists, quotations, alignment helpers, inline markup, a full set of font-size
// declarations, text symbols, floating figures and tables
// with numbered captions, minipages, margin notes, a numbered reference list with
// citations into it, an index, and a couple of conditionals. This single
// script exercises every public command, environment, and declaration the
// package provides, framed as a short article about itself.

\use{document}

// ---- Title block ---------------------------------------------------------------
// \title / \author / \date record their argument; \maketitle typesets them,
// centred, at the top of the document.
\title {A Tour of the \TeXish{} Article Format}
\author {The texish project}
\date {June 2026}
\maketitle

// The abstract environment sets a centred bold "Abstract" heading over a smaller,
// narrower block.
\begin{abstract}
This document exercises the full feature set of the \mono{document} package:
the title block and abstract, numbered and unnumbered sectioning, a table of
contents whose page numbers resolve in a second pass, lists of every kind,
quotations, alignment, inline markup, the LaTeX font-size declarations, the
common text symbols, floating figures and tables with
numbered captions, minipages, and conditionals. It is set in a 10-point serif
body on a tight \LaTeX{} type scale.
\end{abstract}

// ---- Table of contents ---------------------------------------------------------
// \contents prints a "Contents" heading and lists every numbered heading with the
// page it lands on. The CLI runs the document twice automatically, so the page
// numbers and section numbers shown here resolve correctly.
\contents

\vfill\eject

// ---- Sectioning ----------------------------------------------------------------
\section {Sectioning}\index{sectioning}

The \mono{section}, \mono{subsection} and \mono{subsubsection} commands give
three numbered levels. Numbering is automatic: a subsection resets to one at the
start of each new section, and a subsubsection resets within each subsection.

Notice that this first paragraph after a heading is set flush to the left margin,
with no first-line indent, exactly as in a LaTeX \italic{article}. The paragraphs
that follow, like this one, open with the usual first-line indent and there is no
extra vertical space between them — the indent alone marks the boundary.

\subsection {Subsections}

A subsection sits one level down and carries a two-part number such as 1.1. Its
number is reset every time a new section begins.

\subsubsection {Subsubsections}

A subsubsection is the deepest numbered level, numbered 1.1.1, and is reset within
each subsection.

\subsection {Starred (unnumbered) headings}

Every heading has a starred form that skips the counter and prints no number, and
is left out of the table of contents.

\subsubsection* {An unnumbered subsubsection}

This heading was written with a star, so it stands out from the body without a
number and without a contents entry.

\paragraph{A run-in heading.} The \mono{paragraph} command is the lowest level:
it sets its title bold on the same line as the text it introduces, with no number
and no break after it. The body continues immediately, as you see here.

\subparagraph{And a level below that.} The \mono{subparagraph} command is the same
run-in heading one step down, indented from the margin so that the two levels read
as a hierarchy rather than as one level used twice.

\section {Lists}\index{lists}

The package provides three list environments, all of which nest cleanly.

\subsection {Enumerated lists}

The \mono{enumerate} environment numbers its items automatically \dots

\begin{enumerate}
\item Like this,
\item and like this, long enough to wrap onto a second line so the continuation
aligns under the text rather than the number,
\item and a third point.
\end{enumerate}

Nested lists change their label with depth, as in LaTeX: arabic numbers at the top
level, then bracketed letters, then lowercase roman numerals, then capitals.

\begin{enumerate}
\item The first level counts 1, 2, 3.

\begin{enumerate}
\item The second level letters its items,

\item like this,

\begin{enumerate}
\item and the third level numbers them in lowercase roman,

\begin{enumerate}
\item with capital letters at the fourth.
\end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate}

\item Back at the first level, the numbering carries on from where it left off —
each level counts on its own, so an inner list never disturbs the one around it.
\end{enumerate}

Each label is a macro of its own — \mono{labelenumi} through \mono{labelenumiv} for
the numbers, \mono{labelitemi} through \mono{labelitemiv} for the bullets — so
redefining one changes that level throughout the document.

\subsection {Bulleted and nested lists}

The \mono{itemize} environment marks each item with a bullet, and lists nest to
any depth — a list inside an item simply indents one level further:

\begin{itemize}
\item a bullet,

\item another bullet, with text long enough to wrap so the continuation lines
align neatly under the text rather than under the marker,

\item including a nested list inside an item:

\begin{itemize}
\item a nested bullet,

\item and another nested bullet, also long enough to wrap, so the deeper hanging
indent stands out against the outer level.
\end{itemize}

\item and back out at the first level for the final item.
\end{itemize}

\subsection {Description lists}

The \mono{description} environment pairs a bold term, given to \mono{item} in
brackets, with its hanging definition:

\begin{description}
\item[texish] the layout engine these notes are typeset with.
\item[package] a reusable bundle of macros, loaded with \mono{use}.
\item[document] the article-style package this very script demonstrates.
\end{description}

\section {Quotations}

A \mono{quote} block is indented on both sides and remains fully justified:

\begin{quote}
The art of writing is the art of discovering what you believe. Good prose is like
a windowpane, clear enough that the reader forgets it is there at all and sees
only what lies beyond it.
\end{quote}

A \mono{quotation} block is the same, indented a little wider — suited to longer,
multi-paragraph passages:

\begin{quotation}
Style is not a fixed thing but a way of moving through a sentence. The writer who
attends to rhythm and clarity will find that form and meaning arrive together,
each shaping the other.
\end{quotation}

The text returns to the full measure after either block closes.

\section {Alignment}

The alignment environments set whole blocks centred, flush left, or flush right,
and there are single-line helpers as well.

\begin{center}
This paragraph is centred, with both margins stretching equally so that every line
of the wrapped paragraph finds the middle of the measure.
\end{center}

\begin{flushleft}
This paragraph is set flush left, ragged down its right edge, with no first-line
indent.
\end{flushleft}

\begin{flushright}
This paragraph is set flush right, ragged down its left edge.
\end{flushright}

The single-line helpers \mono{centerline}, \mono{leftline} and
\mono{rightline} place one line each at the left, centre, or right of the
measure:

\centerline{This single line is centred.}
\leftline{This single line is flush left.}
\rightline{This single line is flush right.}

\noindent The \mono{centering} declaration switches the rest of a group to
centred lines, the basis of the \mono{center} environment above.

\section {Inline markup}\index{markup}

The body can be set \italic{emphasised}, \italic{italic}, \slanted{slanted},
\bold{bold}, \smallcaps{in small caps}, or \underline{underlined}, switched between
the family roles \serif{roman}, \sans{sans-serif}, and \mono{typewriter}, and
reset to the plain face with {\normalfont normal}. A water molecule is
H\textsub{2}O and the 1\textsup{st} item carries a raised ordinal.
The ellipsis commands \mono{dots} and \mono{ldots} both set \dots{} three dots.

\section {Font sizes}

The size declarations run the full LaTeX scale, each keeping the current family and
shape and resetting the leading. From smallest to largest:
{\tiny tiny} {\scriptsize scriptsize} {\footnotesize footnotesize} {\small small}
{\normalsize normalsize} {\large large} {\Large Large} {\LARGE LARGE}
{\huge huge} {\Huge Huge}.

\section {Text symbols}

The common LaTeX text symbols each set a single character: the section sign~\S, the
paragraph mark~\P, the dagger~\dag{} and double dagger~\ddag, the copyright
sign~\copyright, the registered mark~\textregistered, the trademark~\texttrademark,
the pound sign~\pounds, a bullet~\textbullet, an em dash~\textemdash, an en
dash~\textendash, and the degree sign~\textdegree.

\section {Floats: figures and tables}\index{floats}

A \mono{figure} detaches its body to the top of a page, and a \mono{caption}
written inside numbers it on the figure counter. The \mono{label} on the caption
lets it be cross-referenced: this is Figure~\ref{fig:frog}.

\figure[h]{
\centerline{\includegraphics[width=0.3\linewidth]{frog.jpg}}
\vskip 6pt
\centerline{\caption{A frog, scaled to a third of the text width.}\label{fig:frog}}
}

A \mono{table} float works the same way on its own counter: its caption reads
"Table~1", not "Figure~4", and it files into the list of tables. Its body can be
any content — the float, the caption and the counter are the document-package
features; a tabular grid would come from a separate package.

\table[h]{
\centerline{\begin{minipage}{3.2in}
\bold{Sectioning summary.} The three numbered levels are \mono{section},
\mono{subsection} and \mono{subsubsection}, numbered 1, 1.1 and 1.1.1 and
reset within their parent.
\end{minipage}}
\vskip 6pt
\centerline{\caption{The numbered sectioning levels.}\label{tab:levels}}
}

The two captioned floats above are the source for the lists of figures and tables
on the next page (see Table~\ref{tab:levels} for the sectioning summary).

\vfill\eject

// Lists of figures and tables, each replaying its captions with page numbers.
\listoffigs

\listoftabs

\section {Boxes and conditionals}

A \mono{minipage} sets its body as a paragraph in a box of a chosen width. Two
side by side, centred, with a gap between them:

\centerline{//
\begin{minipage}{2in}
This is the left minipage. Its text wraps inside a two-inch box, independent of the
box beside it.
\end{minipage}//
\hskip 24
\begin{minipage}{2in}
And this is the right minipage, the same width, set as its own little paragraph.
\end{minipage}}

\medskip

\noindent The \mono{if} command chooses between two branches on a boolean test, with
\mono{=} testing two strings for equality. For example,
\if {\={texish}{texish}}the two strings \italic{are} equal\else they are not\fi
— and \if {\={texish}{tex}}they are equal\else the two strings are \italic{not}
equal\fi.

\section {Margin notes}\index{margin notes}

The \mono{marginpar} command sets a short note in the margin beside the line it is
written on.\marginpar{Like this one, set beside the line that called for it.} The
note takes no room in the text: the line is set, and the page broken, exactly as if
it were not there, so adding one never reflows the paragraph that carries it.

In a two-sided document the note goes in the \italic{outer} margin — the right of a
recto, the left of a verso — so notes sit at the open edges of an opening rather
than down its gutter. \mono{marginparwidth} is the measure, \mono{marginparsep} the
gap from the text, and \mono{marginparstyle} how it is set — the face, and the
ragged right edge a measure this narrow wants.

\section {Citations and the bibliography}\index{citations}

The \mono{thebibliography} environment sets a numbered reference list, and
\mono{cite} names its entries in the running text: the first entry here is
\cite{knuth}, and both together are \cite{knuth lamport}. A citation is an ordinary
cross-reference, so it resolves across passes exactly as \mono{ref} does — a
citation of an entry that appears later in the document shows \mono{??} until the
pass that has seen it, never a stale number.

\appendix

\section {After the appendix}

After \mono{appendix}, the section counter restarts and its number switches to a
letter, so this section is Appendix~A while keeping the same \mono{section}
command. A subsection here follows along as A.1.

\subsection {A lettered subsection}

This subsection reads A.1, built on the appendix's lettered top level.

// ---- Reference list and index ---------------------------------------------------
// \bibitem declares a label under the entry's number, which is what lets \cite above
// resolve to it; \printindex gathers every \index mark in the document into one
// alphabetical list, with the pages each term was marked on.

\begin{thebibliography}
\bibitem{knuth} Knuth, Donald E. \italic{The \TeX{}book}. Addison-Wesley, 1984.
\bibitem{lamport} Lamport, Leslie. \italic{\LaTeX: A Document Preparation System}.
Addison-Wesley, second edition, 1994.
\end{thebibliography}

\printindex

\vfill\eject
